  "hero": {
    "_note": "THE ROSTER AND NOTHING ELSE. This section used to carry Cory's own frame lists as well -- `idle`, `walk`, `attack` and their per-set anchors -- because he was the only hero and his walk sheet was the animation system. All five heroes are single pictures now, so the lists are gone with the sheet and `roster` is the whole of it. `attackImpactFrame` stays: the swing still lands on a frame of a clock even though nothing swaps a texture on it, and the damage timing is a tuned number.",
    "attackImpactFrame": 3,
    "roster": {
      "cory": {
        "base": "hero-cory",
        "powered": "hero-cory-power",
        "walk": null,
        "attack": null
      },
      "courtland": {
        "base": "hero-courtland",
        "powered": "hero-courtland-power",
        "walk": null,
        "attack": null
      },
      "han": {
        "base": "hero-han",
        "powered": "hero-han-power",
        "walk": null,
        "attack": null
      },
      "eli": {
        "base": "hero-eli",
        "powered": "hero-eli-power",
        "walk": null,
        "attack": null
      },
      "bailey": {
        "base": "hero-bailey",
        "powered": "hero-bailey-power",
        "walk": null,
        "attack": null
      }
    },
    "_roster": "EVERY HERO'S ART, in one place. `base` is how it starts, `powered` is what it wears once its health has been below half, and `walk`/`attack` are its frame lists or null. NULL IS THE INTERESTING CASE: a hero with no walk sheet is a single picture that would slide across the field, so Hero.ts gives it a small procedural bob instead. Dropping a real sheet in later is replacing the null with a frame list here -- the bob turns itself off, because it is conditioned on the sheet being absent rather than on a flag someone has to remember."
  },
  "worldMap": {
    "_note": "The world map screen, COMPOSED rather than painted. One tiling background reused at every size, one small card cropped from each level's own plate, and a trail drawn in code from the levels' mapPositions. Adding a level is a row in levels.json and one card here; no art is regenerated.",
    "background": "map-bg",
    "cards": {
      "level1": "card-level1",
      "level2": "card-level2",
      "level3": "card-level3",
      "level4": "card-level4"
    }
  },
  "soldierTiers": {
    "turret-dummy": [
      "unit-dummy-1",
      "unit-dummy-2",
      "unit-dummy-3"
    ]
  },
  "_optional": "Art that may legitimately be absent. A key here is skipped by the loader with a console warning rather than a missing-texture error, and every one belongs to a family with a named fallback -- tests/boot.test.ts enforces that. IT IS NOT A LICENCE TO SHIP WITHOUT THE FILE. `queueArt` requests every key in `files`, optional or not -- it cannot know which are absent without asking the server -- so a listed key whose file is missing is a 404 on every boot for every player, and three of them shipped that way. tests/assets.test.ts now fails on exactly that. The eight icons left here are the tower-panel glyphs, which have a generated stand-in.",
  "_greyable": "A greyscale copy is built for each of these at boot (BootScene, systems/Desaturate.ts) and the HUD swaps to it when the thing is unavailable. THE TEN HERO ICONS ARE HERE BECAUSE OF SLOT 2. It is greyed out until the hero transforms, and a tint cannot say that -- Phaser's tint multiplies, so a tinted icon reads as \"in shadow\" rather than as \"switched off\". It used to read as neither: the key was absent from this list, `greyKey` named a texture that had never been built, the swap was skipped and the button kept its full colour with only a dim tint on it. The word LOCKED being painted into the placeholder art was what made that survivable, and that word is gone with the placeholders.",
  "_fx": "EFFECTS THE GAME DRAWS FOR ITSELF, by role. `spark`, `blast`, `puff` and `muzzle` are combat feedback; `bossBolt` is one boss's projectile; `stunned`, `burn` and `mindControl` are STATUS MARKERS drawn over an enemy for as long as the status lasts, which is why they are here rather than in heroes.json beside the ability that applied them -- a burn is a fact about the enemy, and anything that can set something alight draws the same flame. NOTHING CONTROLS AN ENEMY TODAY: `mindControl` is bound so its art ships and is named, and `enemyMarker` in GameScene draws it the day a power sets `Enemy.controlled`.",
  "_heroHeight": "WHY hero-cory-power CARRIES NO displayHeight. Every other sprite's on-screen height is a manifest field, and CLAUDE.md rule 7 says so. The Rivian is the exception because its height is a decision about ONE HERO rather than about one picture: it is 1.51:1, so at the roster's height it would be wider than the road, and the number that fixes that belongs beside the hero it is about. `heroes.json cory.poweredHeight` is it, and it is the only copy -- a second one here is how the two come to disagree. Hero.ts asks the roster first and falls back to this entry, so a hero with no `poweredHeight` is sized by its art exactly as before, and tests/heroes.test.ts fails if a powered form has a height from neither."
}
